  "content": {
    "id": "p27-q4-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "4",
    "prompt": "The lines $y=3x$ and $y=3x+10$ are parallel. Find the shortest distance between these two lines.\n\nHint: Start with the point $(0,0)$ on the first line.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\sqrt{10}$ units.\n\nFind the perpendicular from the origin to the second line, then calculate its length.",
      "worked": "The shortest distance is perpendicular to the parallel lines. The perpendicular through $O(0,0)$ has gradient $-\\frac13$, so its equation is $y=-\\frac13x$. At its intersection $M$ with the second line,\n$$\\begin{align*}-\\frac13x&=3x+10\\\\-x&=9x+30\\\\-10x&=30\\\\x&=-3\\\\y&=-\\frac13(-3)\\\\y&=1.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $M(-3,1)$, and\n$$\\begin{align*}OM&=\\sqrt{(-3-0)^2+(1-0)^2}\\\\OM&=\\sqrt{10}.\\end{align*}$$\nThe shortest distance is $\\sqrt{10}$ units.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 112
  },
